1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Finding a room is one thing.
Finding someone you can actually live well with is harder.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
I built CoHabby Living Decision Room
to put roommate compatibility first.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The page gives a browser agent six small WebMCP tools.
They read the same state I see. Five can change this page in real time.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:31,000
Each has one clear job.
There is no chatbot behind it and no second agent.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,500
I start with a few everyday habits: budget, timing,
pets, quiet time, and how I want shared space to feel.

6
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:50,000
The agent can read these choices and stage a living plan,
but only I can approve it.

7
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:59,500
Once I approve, the agent finds fictional people
and homes that fit those needs.

8
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:10,000
The person comes first. The room is useful context.
I can select a whole card, or ask why that roommate may fit.

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
This Synergy Lens is the signature WebMCP moment.
It opens from a live match.

10
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,500
It explains three practical reasons
our household rhythms may work.

11
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:32,000
The score is a fixed synthetic fixture.
It never uses protected traits or CoHabby's production model.

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Next, the agent compares two current roommates side by side.

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:48,000
I can scan the Synergy read, rent, move timing,
daily rhythm, and household boundaries.

14
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Then I choose who to greet and whether the note
should sound warm, direct, or casual.

15
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:02,000
The final tool prepares a first hello
using only facts already visible on the page.

16
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,500
I can edit every word.
The agent cannot send it or confirm it.

17
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:17,000
That last click stays mine, and the demo says clearly
that no real message was sent.

18
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Under the hood, every tool has a strict schema,
current-state checks, cancellation, and safe output validation.

19
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Every mutation waits for the visible page to render.

20
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
The source, fixtures, assets, and tests are public.
The footer identifies the exact deployed revision.

21
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,500
This is what WebMCP changes for CoHabby.
The browser handles the structured work.

22
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:53,000
I keep the human decisions. Together, we get from living habits
to a thoughtful roommate introduction on one shared page.
